---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const structuredData={"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"HowTo",name:"GEO evidence workflow",description:"Map plausible generative-search questions to claims, source roles, visible evidence, and maintainable SEO page actions.",step:[
  {"@type":"HowToStep",name:"Name the answerable job",text:"Define what the reader must understand, compare, verify, or complete."},
  {"@type":"HowToStep",name:"Build a claim register",text:"List material claims, boundaries, dates, and the source role required for each."},
  {"@type":"HowToStep",name:"Place evidence on the right page",text:"Strengthen the canonical owner or create a separate evidence asset only when its job and lifecycle differ."},
  {"@type":"HowToStep",name:"Verify and monitor",text:"Test crawlability, visible text, internal links, structured-data parity, corrections, and measured performance."}
]};
---
<BaseLayout title="GEO evidence workflow" description="A conservative GEO workflow for turning fanout questions into visible, source-backed, maintainable web evidence without citation promises." canonicalPath="/geo-evidence-workflow/" pageClass="reference-page" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <header class="reference-hero"><p class="mono-label">Workflow · generative search evidence</p><h1>Build evidence a person can inspect—even if no AI system cites it.</h1><p>GEO is useful here as an evidence and answerability discipline. It does not mean reverse-engineering hidden retrieval, adding special AI markup, or promising citations.</p></header>
  <section class="reference-section split-section"><div><h2>The operational question</h2><p>For each plausible fanout branch, ask which claim must be supported, which source role can support it, and which canonical page should own the explanation. The output is page work and evidence work—not an imitation of a model trace.</p></div><div><h2>The platform boundary</h2><p>Google states that its established SEO fundamentals remain relevant for AI features and that no special schema or AI text file is required. Eligibility never guarantees crawling, indexing, visibility, or citation.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features" rel="external">Google: AI features and your website <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p></div></section>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Evidence register</p><h2>Give every important claim an owner and a failure condition.</h2></div><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="GEO evidence register"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Record</th><th>Decision use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reader job</td><td>Learn, compare, evaluate, implement, troubleshoot, or verify.</td><td>Prevents question variants from becoming separate pages by default.</td></tr><tr><td>Claim</td><td>Exact bounded statement, date, geography, product state, and exceptions.</td><td>Defines what the evidence actually needs to support.</td></tr><tr><td>Source role</td><td>Primary documentation, research, expert guidance, firsthand case, comparison data, or community evidence.</td><td>Matches evidence type to claim type.</td></tr><tr><td>Page owner</td><td>Current canonical URL and visible promise.</td><td>Determines section, extension, merge, asset, new URL, or no action.</td></tr><tr><td>Correction owner</td><td>Person, review trigger, and expiry condition.</td><td>Keeps time-sensitive evidence maintainable.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
  <section class="reference-section split-section"><div><h2>Strengthen the canonical page when…</h2><ul><li>The branch supports the same primary outcome.</li><li>The evidence has the same owner and update cadence.</li><li>The reader’s next step remains unchanged.</li><li>A separate page would repeat the parent’s explanation.</li></ul></div><div><h2>Build a separate evidence asset when…</h2><ul><li>The method, dataset, test, or comparison is useful independently.</li><li>Its provenance and limitations need substantial space.</li><li>It changes on a different schedule.</li><li>Other pages can cite the asset naturally without duplicating it.</li></ul></div></section>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Technical gate</p><h2>Make the evidence crawlable, visible, and congruent.</h2></div><p>Keep important explanations in textual content, connect them with crawlable internal links, and ensure structured data represents what readers can actually see. Schema can describe a page; it cannot manufacture trust or guarantee a search feature.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/sd-policies" rel="external">Google: structured data guidelines <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p><a class="button-link" href="/quellenrollen/">Choose the source role <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section>
</BaseLayout>
